#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# color_convert

#% text
# `color_convert(c, model)` gives the colour c in the colour model `rgb_color`, `hue` or `gray_level`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# c is a named colour or a colour term; the result has machine-real components, with the opacity last when it is not 1.

#% item
# Components outside [0, 1] are clipped first.

#% item
# A gray has hue 0; converting to `gray_level` weighs red, green and blue by 0.299, 0.587 and 0.114.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Orange by hue, saturation and brightness:

#% cell
color_convert(orange, hue)
#% output
#= hue(0.08333333333333333, 1.0, 1.0)

#% text
# A hue as red, green and blue:

#% cell
color_convert(hue(0.75), rgb_color)
#% output
#= rgb_color(0.5, 0.0, 1.0)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Converting there and back gives the colour again:

#% cell
color_convert(color_convert(rgb_color(0.2, 0.6, 0.4), hue), rgb_color)
#% output
#= rgb_color(0.2, 0.6, 0.4)

#% text
# The opacity is kept:

#% cell
color_convert(rgb_color(0, 0, 1, 0.5), gray_level)
#% output
#= gray_level(0.114, 0.5)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Only the three models are known:

#% cell
color_convert(red, cmyk)
#% output
#! failure(argument, color_convert, 2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `rgb_color`, `hue`, `gray_level`, `blend`
